  ByteSizeConverter.strings        — per-surface P1/S10 i18n table (every web t() key + native chrome).

Parity reproduced exactly from the web source (cross-platform value parity, not a "corrected" version):
  num = parseFloat(value) ; if isNaN(num) -> null (grid hidden) ;
  bytes = num * 1024^indexOf(unit) ; conversions = BYTE_UNITS.map((u,i) => fmtNumber(bytes/1024^i, i===0?0:4)) ;
  selected cell (c.unit === unit) -> bg-neon-cyan/10 ring-neon-cyan/30 ; value text stays text-white for all cells.
  Verified outputs (en-US): 1024 KB -> [1,048,576 | 1,024.0000 | 1.0000 | 0.0010 | 0.0000] ;
  1 B -> B "1", KB "0.0010", MB "0.0000" ; 2048 B -> B "2,048", KB "2.0000", MB "0.0020".
  parseFloat fidelity pinned: "12abc"->12, "  3.5 "->3.5, "+4"->4, "1e3"->1000, ".5"->0.5, "5."->5, "1e"->1,
  "1.2.3"->1.2, "0x10"->0, "Infinity"->Inf (then safeNumber->0), ""/"abc"/"   "/"."->nil.

i18n keys extracted from the web source (table "ByteSizeConverter"): "Byte Size", "Byte Size Desc",
"Value", "Unit". The web en.json carries "Value"/"Unit" verbatim and has NO entry for "Byte Size"/
"Byte Size Desc", so i18next renders those keys as-is — the native fallbacks reproduce that exactly
(parity). Native-only chrome adds the unparseable-input hint. The view holds no bare English literals
(all via ByteSizeConverterStrings); the unit symbols B/KB/MB/GB/TB are data, not prose (web array values).

States: this is a pure synchronous compute tool — the web source has NO data fetching (no useQuery), so
loading / error / stale / offline are N/A by parity (no async source exists to be in those states).
The web source's two real branches are reproduced and tested: parseable value -> content (the highlighted
conversion grid); unparseable -> the grid is hidden (native renders a friendly hint, never a blank box).

